/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A signal is band-limited when its Fourier transform X(f) is zero for |f| > fmax, where fmax is the highest frequency in hertz.
   - Sampling theorem.
   - fs = 1/Ts > 2fmax
   - Here fs is the sampling rate in Hz, Ts the sampling interval in s and fmax the highest frequency in the signal in Hz.
   - The inequality is strict.
   - To see why the theorem holds, model the sampler as multiplication by an impulse train p(t) = Σ δ(t − nTs), the sum running over all integers n.
   - xs(t) = Σ x(nTs) δ(t − nTs)
   - Here xs(t) is the impulse-sampled signal and x(nTs) the sample at t = nTs.
   - The Fourier transform of the impulse train is another impulse train, of strength 1/Ts at every multiple of fs.
   - Xs(f) = (1/Ts) Σ X(f − kfs)
*/
